    {"ip": "185.234.218.114", "inferred_role": "lookalike_repo_hosting_endpoint", "first_seen": "2026-03-09T10:05:39+0100", "last_seen": "2026-03-09T10:14:22+0100"}
  ],
  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Resource Development / Establish Accounts", "technique": "T1585.003", "what_happened": "At 2026-03-09 10:02:11 CET, Suricata DNS logged the internal developer workstation 10.44.18.63 resolving a burst of code-hosting hostnames whose left-most labels are typosquatted corporate org names: data.dns.rrname values matching 'corp-internal-fork.github.com', 'corp-it-team-mirror.github.com', 'corp-prod-build.gitlab.com', 'corp-devteam.github.com'. These org names do not exist in the corporate GitHub/GitLab tenant — they are attacker-established accounts impersonating internal teams. The pattern (4 lookalike orgs resolved within ~3 minutes) is the canonical 'developer is about to clone an attacker-established lookalike repo' signature that MITRE T1585.003 (Establish Accounts: Code Repositories) describes as the prelude to a supply-chain compromise.", "chain_reason": "Suricata DNS events with data.srcip=10.44.18.63 and data.dns.rrname containing the wildcard pattern '*corp-*' against github.com / gitlab.com parent domains, none of which match the allowlisted corporate org list. Wazuh rule 'Outbound DNS to non-allowlisted code-hosting org' attached MITRE T1585.003.", "first_seen": "2026-03-09T10:02:11+0100"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Initial Access / Supply Chain Compromise — git clone", "technique": "T1195.001", "what_happened": "At 10:05:39 CET, Suricata HTTP+TLS logged the same data.srcip=10.44.18.63 establishing TLS sessions to data.dstip=185.234.218.114 with data.tls.sni values matching the lookalike hosts, followed by HTTP GET requests to data.url paths ending in '.git/info/refs?service=git-upload-pack' — the exact smart-HTTP handshake a 'git clone' performs. The data.http.user_agent field carried 'git/2.39.2', confirming an actual git client (not a browser) initiated the transfer. Rule 91320 ('Outbound git clone to non-allowlisted org') fired. This is the developer actively cloning the attacker-established lookalike repository.", "chain_reason": "Suricata HTTP events with data.srcip=10.44.18.63, data.dstip=185.234.218.114, data.http.user_agent='git/2.39.2', and data.url containing '/info/refs?service=git-upload-pack'. The git-upload-pack service path is only requested by a git clone/fetch operation. rule.id=91320 with rule.mitre.id T1195.001.", "first_seen": "2026-03-09T10:05:39+0100"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Initial Access / Supply Chain Compromise — trojanised dependency manifest", "technique": "T1195.001", "what_happened": "At 10:10:54 CET, after the clone HTTP transfers completed, Wazuh syslog collection logged the cloned repository content being inspected: a requirements.txt manifest referencing dependency names that mimic legitimate corporate packages — 'corp-auth-helper==0.9.1' (the real package is 'corp-auth'), plus zero-prevalence packages 'exfil-util==0.0.1' and 'pycredstore==1.1.0'. rule.description matched 'suspicious package in cloned dependency manifest'. This confirms the cloned repo carries a trojanised supply-chain payload — typosquatted and 0-prevalence dependency names designed to be silently installed.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh log alert with rule.description containing 'suspicious package' / 'dependency manifest' on agent.name corresponding to the developer workstation, listing package names that do not exist in the corporate package allowlist. Chained to step 2 because the manifest inspection timestamp follows the git clone HTTP completion on the same data.srcip.", "first_seen": "2026-03-09T10:10:54+0100"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Initial Access / Supply Chain Compromise — install from non-corp registry", "technique": "T1195.001", "what_happened": "At 10:14:22 CET the same data.srcip=10.44.18.63 issued outbound HTTPS to pypi.org with data.http.user_agent='pip/23.3.1 python/3.11 Darwin', requesting the JSON metadata endpoints of the suspicious packages ('exfil-util', 'pycredstore', 'corp-auth-helper'). Rule 91321 ('pip/npm install from non-corp registry + lookalike name') fired. This is the developer (or an automated build step in the cloned repo) attempting to resolve and install the trojanised dependencies from the public PyPI registry instead of the corporate artifact proxy — the supply-chain compromise reaching execution-precursor stage.", "chain_reason": "Suricata HTTP events with data.srcip=10.44.18.63, data.http.user_agent matching 'pip/*', data.url containing '/pypi/exfil-util/json', '/pypi/pycredstore/json', '/pypi/corp-auth-helper/json'. rule.id=91321. Chained because the package names match exactly the trojanised manifest from step 3 and the same source workstation.", "first_seen": "2026-03-09T10:14:22+0100"}
  ],
